      "math_academy_id": "q-48721",
      "sequence_position": 1,
      "problem": "For the function $f(x)$ and $g(x)$, you're given the following data:\n\n$$\nf(1)=2,\\,{f}^{′}(1)=-3,\\,g(1)=4,\\,{g}^{′}(1)=2\n$$\n\nCompute $\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))$ at $x=1.$",
      "worked_solution": "Using the product rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x)) & =f(x){g}^{′}(x)+g(x){f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $x=1$, this gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(f(x)g(x))|}_{x=1} & =f(1){g}^{′}(1)+g(1){f}^{′}(1) \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+4\\cdot (-3) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
